module

public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Module.Finiteness.TensorProductPi

@[expose] public section

universe u v

/-!
# Interchanging direct products with tensor: the finitely presented case

Let `R` be a commutative ring and `M` an `R`-module. For a family `(Q α)` of `R`-modules the
canonical map `TensorProduct.piRightHom R R M Q : M ⊗[R] (∀ α, Q α) →ₗ[R] ∀ α, M ⊗[R] Q α` is
bijective for every family exactly when `M` is finitely presented, and the successively weaker
conditions of Stacks 059J already force this in their bijective form.

The argument is the source's. A finite presentation `R^m → R^n → M → 0` gives a diagram with
right exact rows whose two leftmost vertical comparison maps are bijective, because the comparison
map is bijective for a finite free module; so the comparison map for `M` is bijective by a
diagram chase. Conversely, given the condition for `M ⊗[R] R^A → M^A`, Stacks 059J makes `M`
finitely generated, hence a quotient of a finite free `L` by `K = ker π`; the same diagram, now
with the two rightmost verticals bijective, shows `K ⊗[R] R^A → K^A` surjective, so `K` is
finitely generated by Stacks 059J and `M` is finitely presented.

## Main results

* `Stacks.FinitePresentation.PiComparison.solution`: the proof of Stacks 059K, as stated in
  `Module.FinitePresentation.tfae_piRightHom_bijective`.

## Implementation notes

The two diagram chases are isolated as
`Stacks.FinitePresentation.PiComparison.bijective_of_surjective_of_bijective` and
`Stacks.FinitePresentation.PiComparison.surjective_of_surjective_of_injective`, the four lemma for
modules in the two forms the proof uses. Mathlib has the four lemma only for abelian categories
(`CategoryTheory.Abelian.isIso_of_epi_of_isIso` and its neighbours); the element-level statements
here are short enough that transporting the diagram to `ModuleCat R` would cost more than it saves.

The comparison map for a finite free module is handled through
`Stacks.FinitePresentation.PiComparison.piScalarLeft`, the isomorphism
`(ι → R) ⊗[R] X ≃ₗ[R] (ι → X)` for finite `ι`. Mathlib has this only on the other side, as
`TensorProduct.piScalarRight`, so it is built here from that and `TensorProduct.comm`.

The passage from item 3 to item 4 tests item 3 on `Shrink.{v} R`, which is where `Small.{v} R` is
used: item 3 quantifies over modules in `Type v` while `R` lives in `Type u`, and
`Shrink.linearEquiv` transports the resulting bijectivity back to the constant family `R`. The
same hypothesis provides the finite free module `L` of `Type v` in the last implication, so that
`K = ker π` lies in `Type v` and Stacks 059J applies to it with test data in `Type v`.

## References

* [StacksProject 059K](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/059K)
-/



namespace Stacks.FinitePresentation.PiComparison

open Function LinearMap TensorProduct

/-! ### The four lemma for modules -/

section FourLemma

variable {R : Type*} [Ring R] {M₁ M₂ M₃ N₁ N₂ N₃ : Type*}
  [AddCommGroup M₁] [Module R M₁] [AddCommGroup M₂] [Module R M₂] [AddCommGroup M₃] [Module R M₃]
  [AddCommGroup N₁] [Module R N₁] [AddCommGroup N₂] [Module R N₂] [AddCommGroup N₃] [Module R N₃]
  {f : M₁ →ₗ[R] M₂} {g : M₂ →ₗ[R] M₃} {f' : N₁ →ₗ[R] N₂} {g' : N₂ →ₗ[R] N₃}
  {c₁ : M₁ →ₗ[R] N₁} {c₂ : M₂ →ₗ[R] N₂} {c₃ : M₃ →ₗ[R] N₃}

/-- In a commutative diagram of `R`-modules
```
M₁ --f--> M₂ --g--> M₃ --> 0
|c₁       |c₂       |c₃
N₁ --f'-> N₂ --g'-> N₃ --> 0
```
with right exact rows, if `c₁` is surjective and `c₂` is bijective then `c₃` is bijective. -/
private theorem bijective_of_surjective_of_bijective
    (sq₁ : c₂ ∘ₗ f = f' ∘ₗ c₁) (sq₂ : c₃ ∘ₗ g = g' ∘ₗ c₂)
    (hgf : g ∘ₗ f = 0) (hg : Surjective g) (hg' : Surjective g') (hex' : Exact f' g')
    (h₁ : Surjective c₁) (h₂ : Bijective c₂) : Bijective c₃ := by
  refine ⟨(injective_iff_map_eq_zero c₃).2 fun z hz => ?_, fun z' => ?_⟩
  · obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := hg z
    have h0 : g' (c₂ y) = 0 := by rw [← LinearMap.comp_apply, ← sq₂, LinearMap.comp_apply, hz]
    obtain ⟨w', hw'⟩ := (hex' _).mp h0
    obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := h₁ w'
    have h1 : c₂ (f w) = c₂ y := by rw [← LinearMap.comp_apply, sq₁, LinearMap.comp_apply, hw']
    rw [← h₂.1 h1]
    simpa using congr($hgf w)
  · obtain ⟨y', rfl⟩ := hg' z'
    obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := h₂.2 y'
    exact ⟨g y, congr($sq₂ y)⟩

/-- In a commutative diagram of `R`-modules
```
M₁ --f--> M₂ --g--> M₃
|c₁       |c₂       |c₃
N₁ --f'-> N₂ --g'-> N₃
```
whose top row is exact at `M₂`, whose bottom row is a complex with `f'` injective, if `c₂` is
surjective and `c₃` is injective then `c₁` is surjective. -/
private theorem surjective_of_surjective_of_injective
    (sq₁ : c₂ ∘ₗ f = f' ∘ₗ c₁) (sq₂ : c₃ ∘ₗ g = g' ∘ₗ c₂)
    (hex : Exact f g) (hgf' : g' ∘ₗ f' = 0) (hf' : Injective f')
    (h₂ : Surjective c₂) (h₃ : Injective c₃) : Surjective c₁ := by
  intro y₁
  obtain ⟨x₂, hx₂⟩ := h₂ (f' y₁)
  have hgx : g x₂ = 0 := by
    refine h₃ ?_
    rw [map_zero, ← LinearMap.comp_apply, sq₂, LinearMap.comp_apply, hx₂, ← LinearMap.comp_apply,
      hgf', LinearMap.zero_apply]
  obtain ⟨x₁, rfl⟩ := (hex _).mp hgx
  exact ⟨x₁, hf' (by rw [← LinearMap.comp_apply, ← sq₁]; exact hx₂)⟩

end FourLemma

/-! ### Products of exact sequences -/

section Pi

variable {R : Type*} [Ring R] {ι : Type*} {φ ψ χ : ι → Type*}
  [∀ i, AddCommGroup (φ i)] [∀ i, Module R (φ i)] [∀ i, AddCommGroup (ψ i)] [∀ i, Module R (ψ i)]
  [∀ i, AddCommGroup (χ i)] [∀ i, Module R (χ i)]

/-- A product of exact pairs of linear maps is exact. -/
private theorem exact_piMap {f : ∀ i, φ i →ₗ[R] ψ i} {g : ∀ i, ψ i →ₗ[R] χ i}
    (h : ∀ i, Exact (f i) (g i)) : Exact (piMap f) (piMap g) := by
  intro y
  simp only [LinearMap.coe_piMap, Pi.map_apply, funext_iff, Pi.zero_apply]
  refine ⟨fun hy => ?_, ?_⟩
  · choose x hx using fun i => (h i _).mp (hy i)
    exact ⟨x, funext hx⟩
  · rintro ⟨x, rfl⟩ i
    exact (h i _).mpr ⟨x i, rfl⟩

/-- A product of surjective linear maps is surjective. -/
private theorem surjective_piMap {f : ∀ i, φ i →ₗ[R] ψ i} (h : ∀ i, Surjective (f i)) :
    Surjective (piMap f) := fun y => by
  choose x hx using fun i => h i (y i)
  exact ⟨x, funext hx⟩

end Pi

/-! ### The comparison maps -/

section Comparison

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R]

/-- The isomorphism `(ι → R) ⊗[R] X ≃ₗ[R] (ι → X)` for a finite index type `ι`, the left-hand
analogue of `TensorProduct.piScalarRight`. -/
private noncomputable def piScalarLeft (ι : Type*) [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
    (X : Type*) [AddCommGroup X] [Module R X] : (ι → R) ⊗[R] X ≃ₗ[R] (ι → X) :=
  (TensorProduct.comm R (ι → R) X).trans (piScalarRight R R X ι)

private lemma piScalarLeft_tmul {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
    {X : Type*} [AddCommGroup X] [Module R X] (a : ι → R) (x : X) (i : ι) :
    piScalarLeft ι X (a ⊗ₜ x) i = a i • x := by
  simp [piScalarLeft]

variable {A : Type*} (Q : A → Type*) [∀ α, AddCommGroup (Q α)] [∀ α, Module R (Q α)]

/-- `TensorProduct.piRightHom` is natural in the left tensor factor. -/
private lemma piRightHom_comp_rTensor {N₁ N₂ : Type*} [AddCommGroup N₁] [Module R N₁]
    [AddCommGroup N₂] [Module R N₂] (h : N₁ →ₗ[R] N₂) :
    piRightHom R R N₂ Q ∘ₗ rTensor (∀ α, Q α) h
      = piMap (fun α => rTensor (Q α) h) ∘ₗ piRightHom R R N₁ Q := by
  ext n q α
  simp

/-- `TensorProduct.piScalarRightHom` is natural in the left tensor factor. -/
private lemma piScalarRightHom_comp_rTensor (B : Type*) {N₁ N₂ : Type*} [AddCommGroup N₁]
    [Module R N₁] [AddCommGroup N₂] [Module R N₂] (h : N₁ →ₗ[R] N₂) :
    piScalarRightHom R R N₂ B ∘ₗ rTensor (B → R) h
      = h.compLeft B ∘ₗ piScalarRightHom R R N₁ B := by
  ext n q α
  simp

/-- `TensorProduct.piScalarRightHom` is `TensorProduct.piRightHom` for the constant family `R`. -/
private lemma piScalarRightHom_eq (B : Type*) (N : Type*) [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] :
    (piScalarRightHom R R N B : _ → _)
      = (fun (h : B → N ⊗[R] R) α => TensorProduct.rid R N (h α))
          ∘ (piRightHom R R N fun _ : B => R : _ → _) := by
  funext x
  funext α
  induction x using TensorProduct.inductionOn with
  | tmul y p => simp
  | add x y hx hy => simp_all

/-- `TensorProduct.piRightHom` is natural in the family of right tensor factors. -/
private lemma piRightHom_comp_lTensor {Q' : A → Type*} [∀ α, AddCommGroup (Q' α)]
    [∀ α, Module R (Q' α)] {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] (e : ∀ α, Q α →ₗ[R] Q' α) :
    piRightHom R R N Q' ∘ₗ lTensor N (LinearMap.pi fun α => e α ∘ₗ LinearMap.proj α)
      = piMap (fun α => lTensor N (e α)) ∘ₗ piRightHom R R N Q := by
  ext n q α
  simp

/-- Bijectivity transfers along a commuting square whose horizontal maps are equivalences. -/
private lemma bijective_of_comp_eq {X Y Z W : Type*} {c₁ : X → Y} {c₂ : Z → W} (e : X ≃ Z)
    (e' : Y ≃ W) (key : c₂ ∘ e = e' ∘ c₁) (h : Bijective c₁) : Bijective c₂ := by
  refine (Bijective.of_comp_iff _ e.bijective).1 ?_
  rw [key]
  exact e'.bijective.comp h

/-- Bijectivity of `TensorProduct.piRightHom` transfers along a linear equivalence in the left
tensor factor. -/
private lemma bijective_piRightHom_of_linearEquiv {N₁ N₂ : Type*} [AddCommGroup N₁] [Module R N₁]
    [AddCommGroup N₂] [Module R N₂] (e : N₁ ≃ₗ[R] N₂)
    (h : Bijective (piRightHom R R N₁ Q)) : Bijective (piRightHom R R N₂ Q) :=
  bijective_of_comp_eq (e.rTensor (∀ α, Q α)).toEquiv
    (Equiv.piCongrRight fun α => (e.rTensor (Q α)).toEquiv)
    congr(⇑$(piRightHom_comp_rTensor Q (e : N₁ →ₗ[R] N₂))) h

/-- Bijectivity of `TensorProduct.piRightHom` transfers along a linear equivalence in the family
of right tensor factors. -/
private lemma bijective_piRightHom_of_linearEquiv_right {Q' : A → Type*}
    [∀ α, AddCommGroup (Q' α)] [∀ α, Module R (Q' α)] {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N]
    (e : ∀ α, Q α ≃ₗ[R] Q' α) (h : Bijective (piRightHom R R N Q)) :
    Bijective (piRightHom R R N Q') :=
  bijective_of_comp_eq ((LinearEquiv.piCongrRight e).lTensor N).toEquiv
    (Equiv.piCongrRight fun α => ((e α).lTensor N).toEquiv)
    congr(⇑$(piRightHom_comp_lTensor Q fun α => (e α : Q α →ₗ[R] Q' α))) h

/-- The comparison map for a finite free module, computed on the basis `Pi.basisFun`. -/
private lemma piScalarLeft_piRightHom {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
    (x : (ι → R) ⊗[R] (∀ α, Q α)) (α : A) (i : ι) :
    piScalarLeft ι (Q α) (piRightHom R R (ι → R) Q x α) i = piScalarLeft ι (∀ α, Q α) x i α := by
  induction x using TensorProduct.inductionOn with
  | tmul y p => simp [piScalarLeft_tmul]
  | add x y hx hy => simp_all

/-- The comparison map is bijective for a free module with a finite basis. -/
private lemma bijective_piRightHom_of_basis {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N]
    {ι : Type*} [Finite ι] (b : Module.Basis ι R N) : Bijective (piRightHom R R N Q) := by
  classical
  have : Fintype ι := Fintype.ofFinite ι
  refine bijective_piRightHom_of_linearEquiv Q b.equivFun.symm ?_
  have key : (piRightHom R R (ι → R) Q : _ → _) =
      (Equiv.piCongrRight fun α => (piScalarLeft ι (Q α)).toEquiv.symm)
        ∘ (Equiv.piComm fun (_ : ι) (α : A) => Q α) ∘ (piScalarLeft ι (∀ α, Q α)) := by
    funext x
    funext α
    simp only [comp_apply, Equiv.piCongrRight_apply, LinearEquiv.coe_toEquiv_symm]
    exact (((piScalarLeft ι (Q α)).toEquiv.symm_apply_eq).2
      (funext fun i => (piScalarLeft_piRightHom Q x α i).symm)).symm
  rw [key]
  exact ((Equiv.piCongrRight fun α => (piScalarLeft ι (Q α)).toEquiv.symm).bijective.comp
    (Equiv.piComm _).bijective).comp (piScalarLeft ι (∀ α, Q α)).bijective

/-- The comparison map `N ⊗[R] R^B → N^B` is bijective for a free module `N` with a finite
basis. -/
private lemma bijective_piScalarRightHom_of_basis (B : Type*) {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N]
    [Module R N] {ι : Type*} [Finite ι] (b : Module.Basis ι R N) :
    Bijective (piScalarRightHom R R N B) := by
  rw [piScalarRightHom_eq]
  exact (Equiv.piCongrRight fun _ : B => (TensorProduct.rid R N).toEquiv).bijective.comp
    (bijective_piRightHom_of_basis _ b)

end Comparison

/-! ### The two implications with content -/

section Main

variable (R : Type u) [CommRing R]

/-- (1) ⇒ (2): the comparison map is bijective for a finitely presented module. -/
private lemma bijective_piRightHom_of_finitePresentation (M : Type*) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    [Module.FinitePresentation R M] {A : Type*} (Q : A → Type*) [∀ α, AddCommGroup (Q α)]
    [∀ α, Module R (Q α)] : Bijective (piRightHom R R M Q) := by
  obtain ⟨n, m, f, g, hf, hex⟩ := Module.FinitePresentation.exists_fin' R M
  refine bijective_of_surjective_of_bijective (f := rTensor (∀ α, Q α) g)
    (g := rTensor (∀ α, Q α) f) (f' := piMap fun α => rTensor (Q α) g)
    (g' := piMap fun α => rTensor (Q α) f) (piRightHom_comp_rTensor Q g)
    (piRightHom_comp_rTensor Q f) ?_ (rTensor_surjective _ hf)
    (surjective_piMap fun α => rTensor_surjective (Q α) hf)
    (exact_piMap fun α => rTensor_exact (Q α) hex hf)
    (bijective_piRightHom_of_basis Q (Pi.basisFun R (Fin m))).2
    (bijective_piRightHom_of_basis Q (Pi.basisFun R (Fin n)))
  rw [← rTensor_comp, hex.linearMap_comp_eq_zero, rTensor_zero]

/-- (4) ⇒ (1): a module for which `M ⊗[R] R^A → M^A` is bijective for every `A : Type v` is
finitely presented. -/
private lemma finitePresentation_of_bijective_piScalarRightHom [Small.{v} R] (M : Type v)
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (h : ∀ A : Type v, Bijective (piScalarRightHom R R M A)) :
    Module.FinitePresentation R M := by
  have hM : Module.Finite R M :=
    ((Module.Finite.tfae_piRightHom_surjective R M).out 4 1).mp fun A => (h A).2
  -- a finite free module of `Type v` surjecting onto `M`; this is where `Small.{v} R` is used
  obtain ⟨L, _, _, k, b, π, hπ⟩ :
      ∃ (L : Type v) (_ : AddCommGroup L) (_ : Module R L) (k : ℕ)
        (_ : Module.Basis (Fin k) R L) (π : L →ₗ[R] M), Surjective π := by
    obtain ⟨n, s, hs⟩ := Module.Finite.exists_fin (R := R) (M := M)
    refine ⟨Fin n → Shrink.{v} R, inferInstance, inferInstance, n,
      (Pi.basisFun R (Fin n)).map
        (LinearEquiv.piCongrRight fun _ => Shrink.linearEquiv R R).symm,
      Fintype.linearCombination R s ∘ₗ
        (LinearEquiv.piCongrRight fun _ => Shrink.linearEquiv R R).toLinearMap, fun x => ?_⟩
    obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := (Submodule.mem_span_range_iff_exists_fun R).mp
      (hs ▸ (Submodule.mem_top : x ∈ (⊤ : Submodule R M)))
    refine ⟨(LinearEquiv.piCongrRight fun _ => Shrink.linearEquiv R R).symm c, ?_⟩
    rw [LinearMap.comp_apply, LinearEquiv.coe_coe, LinearEquiv.apply_symm_apply,
      Fintype.linearCombination_apply]
    exact hc
  have : Module.Free R L := Module.Free.of_basis b
  have : Module.Finite R L := Module.Finite.of_basis b
  refine Module.finitePresentation_of_free_of_surjective π hπ ?_
  -- `ker π` is finitely generated, by Stacks 059J applied to it
  rw [← Module.Finite.iff_fg]
  refine ((Module.Finite.tfae_piRightHom_surjective R (LinearMap.ker π)).out 4 1).mp fun A => ?_
  refine surjective_of_surjective_of_injective
    (f := rTensor (A → R) (LinearMap.ker π).subtype) (g := rTensor (A → R) π)
    (f' := (LinearMap.ker π).subtype.compLeft A) (g' := π.compLeft A)
    (piScalarRightHom_comp_rTensor A _) (piScalarRightHom_comp_rTensor A π)
    (rTensor_exact _ (exact_subtype_ker_map π) hπ) ?_ ?_
    (bijective_piScalarRightHom_of_basis A b).2 (h A).1
  · refine LinearMap.ext fun y => funext fun i => ?_
    simp
  · intro y z hyz
    exact funext fun i => Subtype.ext (by simpa using congrFun hyz i)

end Main

/-- **Stacks 059K.** For an `R`-module `M`, finite presentation is equivalent to bijectivity of the
comparison map `M ⊗[R] (∀ α, Q α) → ∀ α, M ⊗[R] Q α` for every family `Q`, for every constant
family `Q^A`, and for the family `R^A`, the test data living in the universe of `M`. -/
public theorem solution (R : Type u) [CommRing R]
    [Small.{v} R] (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] :
    List.TFAE
      [Module.FinitePresentation R M,
        ∀ (A : Type v) (Q : A → Type v) [∀ α, AddCommGroup (Q α)] [∀ α, Module R (Q α)],
          Function.Bijective (TensorProduct.piRightHom R R M Q),
        ∀ (A : Type v) (Q : Type v) [AddCommGroup Q] [Module R Q],
          Function.Bijective (TensorProduct.piRightHom R R M fun _ : A ↦ Q),
        ∀ A : Type v, Function.Bijective (TensorProduct.piScalarRightHom R R M A)] := by
  tfae_have 1 → 2 := fun _ A Q _ _ =>
    bijective_piRightHom_of_finitePresentation R M Q
  tfae_have 2 → 3 := fun h A Q _ _ => h A fun _ => Q
  tfae_have 3 → 4 := by
    intro h A
    have h1 : Bijective (piRightHom R R M fun _ : A => Shrink.{v} R) := h A (Shrink.{v} R)
    have h2 : Bijective (piRightHom R R M fun _ : A => R) :=
      bijective_piRightHom_of_linearEquiv_right _ (fun _ => Shrink.linearEquiv R R) h1
    rw [piScalarRightHom_eq]
    exact (Equiv.piCongrRight fun _ : A => (TensorProduct.rid R M).toEquiv).bijective.comp h2
  tfae_have 4 → 1 := finitePresentation_of_bijective_piScalarRightHom R M
  tfae_finish

end Stacks.FinitePresentation.PiComparison

/-!
# Interchanging direct products with tensor: the finitely presented case

Let `R` be a commutative ring, let `M` be an `R`-module and let `(Q α)` be a family of
`R`-modules indexed by a set `A`. There is a canonical map
`M ⊗[R] (∀ α, Q α) → ∀ α, M ⊗[R] Q α`, given on pure tensors by `x ⊗ (q α) ↦ (x ⊗ q α)`;
it is an isomorphism when `A` is finite, and in general it is neither injective nor surjective.
Proposition 059J characterizes finite generation of `M` by surjectivity of this map; this file
states that *bijectivity* of the same map characterizes finite presentation, and that the three
successively weaker conditions of 059J already do so in their bijective form: bijectivity for
every family, for every constant family `Q^A`, and bijectivity of `M ⊗[R] R^A → M^A` for every
set `A`.

Finite presentation gives the first because a presentation `R^m → R^n → M → 0` produces a diagram
whose rows are exact and whose first two vertical comparison maps are isomorphisms, the case of a
finite index type; the comparison map for `M` is then surjective, and injective by a diagram
chase. Conversely, if `M ⊗[R] R^A → M^A` is bijective for every `A`, then `M` is finitely
generated by 059J, so it is the quotient of a finite free module `F` by some `K`; comparing
`K ⊗[R] R^A → F ⊗[R] R^A → M ⊗[R] R^A` with `K^A → F^A → M^A`, where the last two vertical maps
are isomorphisms, shows `K ⊗[R] R^A → K^A` surjective, whence `K` too is finitely generated by
059J.

## Main results

* `Module.FinitePresentation.tfae_piRightHom_bijective`: for an `R`-module `M`, the following are
  equivalent: `M` is finitely presented; `TensorProduct.piRightHom` is bijective for every family
  of `R`-modules; it is bijective for every constant family; and `TensorProduct.piScalarRightHom`,
  the map `M ⊗[R] R^A → M^A`, is bijective for every set `A`.

## Implementation notes

The four conditions are the four conditions of `Module.Finite.tfae_piRightHom_surjective` (Stacks
059J) with `Function.Surjective` replaced by `Function.Bijective` and finite generation by finite
presentation, and they are listed in the source's order, so that
`(Module.FinitePresentation.tfae_piRightHom_bijective R M).out 1 4` is the source's `(1) ↔ (4)`
(`List.TFAE.out` counts from `1`).
The equivalence is stated as a `List.TFAE`, as the source states it, each item quantifying over
its own test data. Bijectivity of an `R`-linear map is the source's "bijective"; the statement
does not package it as a `LinearEquiv`, so that the four items differ from 059J's in one word.

The canonical maps the source names are pinned to Mathlib's, not merely asserted to exist. The
comparison map of the section is `TensorProduct.piRightHom R R M Q`, whose value on `x ⊗ q` is
`fun α ↦ x ⊗ q α`, and the source's `M ⊗_R Q^A → (M ⊗_R Q)^A` is that map for the constant family
`fun _ : A ↦ Q`. The source's `M ⊗_R R^A → M^A` is `TensorProduct.piScalarRightHom R R M A`, whose
value on `x ⊗ a` is `fun α ↦ a α • x`; this is the composite of `piRightHom` for the constant
family `R` with the identification `M ⊗[R] R = M`, so no identification is left implicit.

The index types and the test modules range over `Type v`, the universe of `M`, exactly as in
059J, so that the items of the two propositions match one another index for index and 059J can be
cited as it stands. Unlike 059J, this requires `Small.{v} R`: the hypothesis is what makes the
kernel `K` of a surjection from a finite free module available in `Type v`, where 059J can be
applied to it. It cannot be dropped. For a valuation ring `R` whose value group has positive part
of coinitiality larger than every cardinal of `Type v` and `M` its residue field `R ⧸ 𝔪`, every
family `(a α) : A → 𝔪` with `A : Type v` has a common lower bound and hence lies in a principal
ideal, so `M ⊗[R] R^A → M^A` is bijective for every `A : Type v`, while `M` is not finitely
presented, `𝔪 = Module.annihilator R M` not being finitely generated. The hypothesis is automatic
when `R` itself lives in `Type v`, by `small_self`, and it is the hypothesis carried by the
neighbouring Lemmas 059D and 059L. A family of modules in a larger universe is reached by
applying the statement to a `ULift` copy of `M`.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is idle: `Small.{v} R` is what the implication from item 4 to item
1 needs, as the valuation ring above shows, and both `R` and `M` are used by every item, nothing
else being assumed of either — no finiteness, no Noetherian hypothesis — the test data of items 2,
3 and 4 being quantified inside those items. (2) The scalars and the base are the source's: every
tensor product is over `R`, every comparison map is `R`-linear, and item 1 is finite presentation
of `M` as an `R`-module; no localization and no base change occurs. (3) The canonical maps are
pinned, as described above, to `TensorProduct.piRightHom R R M Q` and
`TensorProduct.piScalarRightHom R R M A`; the assertion is bijectivity of those maps, not the
existence of some isomorphism. (4) The instantiation is not degenerate and both directions have
content: for `R = ℤ` and `M = ℤ ⧸ 2ℤ` all four conditions hold, and for `R = ℤ` and `M = ℚ` all
four fail, item 4 already at `A = ℕ` by the source's Example 059I. Finite generation alone does
not suffice, so the four items say strictly more than those of 059J: for `R = ℤ[x₁, x₂, …]` and
`M = R ⧸ (x₁, x₂, …)` the map `M ⊗[R] R^ℕ → M^ℕ` is surjective but not injective, `n ↦ xₙ` being a
family in `Module.annihilator R M` lying in no finitely generated subideal.

